05 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537205 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537205 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "119140e4-3b93-53de-ab7b-40456eb040a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538006 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538006 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3418e5df-6b11-57d2-bb99-1913431eb137" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538006 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186481357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538032 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538032 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "876ec9b0-fca4-5f08-ab51-9230e03dee9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538032 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186481411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538051 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538051 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bd571a32-8693-5c5f-99bb-0070c9bb2fcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538051 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cb0bae8e-56e4-5d43-8dbc-3436ea5c01f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186281662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538069 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186538006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538069 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f52a63f9-5aaf-58b5-bc92-454c75b1e996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538069 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186281649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538132 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538107 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=2 x y^{2} + 2 y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10952efb-f91e-5efa-8836-2bdfa5e4547d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538107 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},5,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,3,1)$ and $(e^{2},5,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},5,e^{-2})-f(1,3,1) \\\\  & =\\left(2 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(5\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(3\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(24\\right) \\\\  & =-24 + \\frac{10}{e^{2}} + 50 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538122 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538127 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538122 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538122 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=x y^{2} + 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538122 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "55b9003c-2f9b-5f89-8acf-67ac21d03597" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538122 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,2,e^{-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,1,1)$ and $(e,2,e^{-1}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,2,e^{-1})-f(1,1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(e^{-1}\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6 + 4 e\\right)-\\left(4\\right) \\\\  & =2 + 4 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538128 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538116 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538128 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538128 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=- x y^{2} - 2 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 3,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538128 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f288c44-ccaa-54db-bd90-19654202c121" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538128 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},5,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,3,1)$ and $(e^{2},5,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},5,e^{-2})-f(1,3,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(5\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)^{2} - 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 25 e^{2} - 10\\right)-\\left(-15\\right) \\\\  & =5 - 25 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538148 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538137 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{2} z - 2 x \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},3 t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb2e2e55-8936-54d7-a33c-03469d398524" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538137 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2 \\pi) & =⟨3,0,6 \\pi⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(3,0,0)$ and $(3,0,6 \\pi).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2 \\pi))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(3,0,6 \\pi)-f(3,0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(6 \\pi\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(3\\right)^{2} \\left(0\\right) - 2 \\left(3\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\left(\\left(54 \\pi+0\\right)-\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =- 108 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538126 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538139 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y^{2} + x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 1,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd1bb89c-9ca9-5d27-95e0-43a9165543a7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538139 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,2,e^{-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,1,1)$ and $(e,2,e^{-1}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,2,e^{-1})-f(1,1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(e\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(e^{-1}\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 2 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(2 - 8 e\\right)-\\left(-1\\right) \\\\  & =3 - 8 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186538119 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538145 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x,y,z)=3 x y^{2} - 3 x y z.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨e^{t},t + 2,e^{- t}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "edc4ddd0-1255-518c-9176-4cb2e8db04ff" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538145 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨e^{2},4,e^{-2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2,1)$ and $(e^{2},4,e^{-2}).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e^{2},4,e^{-2})-f(1,2,1) \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(e^{2}\\right) \\left(4\\right) \\left(e^{-2}\\right)\\right)-\\left(3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right)^{2} - 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(2\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(-12 + 48 e^{2}\\right)-\\left(6\\right) \\\\  & =-18 + 48 e^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538149 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186133878.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538149 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "c0f94114-d881-5eba-937b-16347aa0a14c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186538220 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538220 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538220 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538220 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538220 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186539110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186538220 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #e